Subject: POLI: The future of cryonics

  I've heard rumors that in some states laws are being proposed for
confiscation of organs for donation upon death as "default". In other words,
you have to explicitly state that you do not want to donate your organs
when you die otherwise it is assumed that you want to donate them.
  I brought up this rumor with Harry during a phone chat and we discussed
the implications this would have on cryonics. Could you imagine the
public outcry against cryonics?  Why should you have the right to take
some perfectly good organs to your "grave"?  You shouldn't try to live
by extrordinary means when someone else could benefit from your death.

  I believe cryonics organizations, if they ever become noticed
by the public, can use neurosuspension as a brilliant PR move. Cryonics
suspension of a single patient saves not just one life, but many.
Simply have Alcor start an organ donation program which requires 
neurosuspension patients to donate their organs to save the lives of others.
Secondly, emphasize that cryonics research has allowed the preservation
of organs for longer periods of time saving yet more lives.

Many people find neurosuspension "gross" because of the decapitation aspects
of it, but I believe in time, neurosuspension will become accepted, and perhaps
valued.


