Date: Tue, 29 Dec 92 11:25:08 PST
From: Robin Hanson <hanson@ptolemy.arc.nasa.gov>
Subject: Libel

Pamela Williard and Mark Pickens write:

 I think it would be useful to find a non-coercive way to
 discourage libel.
 
 Currently, a common attitude is:  "If it's a lie, why doesn't he sue?"  
 
 Why not turn this around by suing in Mock Court?  The loser could agree in
 advance to pay court costs and the court could use the same rules of
 evidence and require the same standard of proof (beyond a reasonable doubt)
 as a real court.  Verdicts could be libel proved, not proved, or disproved.
 The only penalty the court could issue (aside from forfeiture of court
 costs) would be a public announcement of that verdict from itself, a
 recognized authority.  
 
 Now the attitude is reversed:  "If he's not lying, why is he afraid to
 defend himself in Mock Court?" 


1) He might respond "Why should I spent my time and resources to defend
   myself of your groundless charge?".  
2) You would have to agree on a court, including choices of rules of 
   evidence, etc.  

The second consideration suggests that this court is just as real as
any private law court, but the agreed punishment for libel is just a public
statement.  Had you agreed to a law which fined libelers, everything
would be the same until the punishment part.  Thus this solution is
not "less coercive", though it still might be a good idea.  

The first consideration can be reduced with idea futures and audit
lotteries (wouldn't you know :-).  You can offer conditional bets that
a particular court would vindicate you if it were to consider your
case.  Using an audit lottery (described in my idea futures paper), the
small stakes bet on both sides would be gambled at odds so that if the
gamble wins, there is plenty of money to pay for court costs, and much
more money at stake on either side.  

If you offer high odds on vindication given a wide varitey of
different courts considering the case, and your opponent is unwilling
to bet against you given any court, this in itself should be damning
of his reputation. 

