Date: Tue, 29 Dec 92 11:54:50 PST
From: Robin Hanson <hanson@ptolemy.arc.nasa.gov>
Subject: Re: PPL: Let's Admit We May Disagree

Marc.Ringuette@GS80.SP.CS.CMU.EDU writes:

   I'll look for contracts which have the following properties if possible:

   1. Unbundled.         [ I want to be able to be selective & shop around. ]
   ...
   Contract enforcement: I'd choose an agency and name it on my contracts,
   in addition to an arbitration agency.

Instead of pre-bundling your enforcement agency, you might do better
to hold an auction right after the crime to see who could do it best
and cheapest, to achieve your desired expected levels of punishment or
restitution.  To deter crime, you just need to commit to doing
something like this, but need not commit to who will do the enforcing.

   National defense & weapons control:  I don't want to own guns or rocket
   launchers, nor do I particularly want to live next to someone who does.
   I'm leaning towards moving somewhere with a limited regional government
   to provide protection and weapons control (which seem to require
   control of physical territory).  I plan to protect myself with
   information and computer networks, not guns.  [ Problem: the
   authorities will have more firepower than I will.  

There are rather large economies of scale in defense against organized
aggression, so I would also prefer to contract with a regional defense
organization.  What I want (besides low premiums) is a low probability
of damage due to war, *and* a low probability of a coup where this
defense takes offense against me in violation of our contract.  So my
problem is to design a contract/institution to get this for me.  

I could sign a contract with some other distant agencies, so that they
would come squash my main agency should it turn against me, but I'm
not sure how workable this is.  Perhaps simpler (can you guess what's
coming? :-) is to just create distant betting markets about the
probability of both war damage and of such a coup.  Yes *I* might not
be able to collect on the bets if such a war or coup happens, but
distant speculators could be safe.  So if I and the other customers
can subsidize the betting market enough to get enough distant
speculators, we customers can use the odds in such markets as a way of
guaging the quality of our defense.

Odds conditional on some other agency being our defense, or on some
other leadership of this agency, could help us customers decide when
to try to seek new leaders, new contracts, or to just skip town.

Robin Hanson

