From: Robin Hanson <hanson@ptolemy.arc.nasa.gov>
Subject: Re: PPL: Let's Admit We May Disagree

Robin Hanson writes (regarding reliable procurement of military defense),
> Perhaps simpler is to just create distant betting markets about the
> probability of both war damage and of such a coup.
> ...we customers can use the odds in such markets as a way of
> gauging the quality of our defense.

Marc.Ringuette@GS80.SP.CS.CMU.EDU writes:
>I wonder, though, whether it wouldn't be more efficient to just hire
>an impartial auditing agency to do this?  

Auditing isn't a general way to get information about organizations;
it only works in a limited context.  Betting odds are more general.

Auditing is now just a way to discourage embezzelment, and is only
possible because of standardization on tedious bean-counting
procedures which can uncover the worst such offenses.  If you can
think of some similar set of procedures which will reliably uncover
tendencies to induce wars or coups, that would be great, but this
seems damn hard to me.  

In contrast, a betting market hires whoever has information, needing
neither standard procedures nor validation of impartiality.

>Hmm.  I guess that means that creating a futures market could be
>considered to be an indirect way of hiring an auditor, where a
>potential auditor who already knows the important information will have
>an incentive to jump right in.  But how does this cut it as a way to
>hire an auditor?  If someone chooses to participate, you get a monetary
>guarantee of accuracy -- "if the auditor is off by 5%, it will cost
>them $5000" -- but depending on your own ability to estimate the
>probability of failure, couldn't setting up the market cost you a _lot_
>of money?  On the other hand, if you're too _good_ at estimating the
>probability of failure, presumably nobody would be willing to
>participate in the market because there's no profit to be had.

There is a way to subsidize a betting market so that you can set the
amount of money to be given away, and this is not sensitive to your
own estimate of the actual probability (subsidize a inventory-based
market-maker - details available).  Thus the customers of a
defense agency can just agree to kick in a 10% extra fee to
subsidize betting.

>I guess I just don't see a good reason why an idea futures market will
>give good predictions, until it has a whole lot of money in it (say,
>$1M to $10M).  And in that case, it sounds like a potentially expensive
>way of hiring an auditor.

$1M, even per year, sounds cheap to me if it can give you accurate
information about the quality of your regional defense agency.
Defense is important, and perhaps the major expense *is* keeping it
off the backs of those being defended.  Many (most?) countries in the
world today have dramatic reductions in their living standards because
of local military "corruption".

-- Marc Ringuette (mnr@cs.cmu.edu)


