Date: Wed, 30 Dec 1992 00:01:33 -0800
From: Pamela Williard and Mark Pickens <autarch@well.sf.ca.us>
Subject: Libel

I don't mean to belabor the point, Robin, but I can't let this pass.

> If you are forced to use a particular Mock Court, spending your time
> following their rules and procedures, that's coercive too.  Libel
> punishable by fine or official statement is coercion, if you did not
> agree to the forum.  But either type of libel punishment is not
> coercion, if you did agree to the forum.  Thus the type of punishment
> is not the relevant factor in deciding if coercion is involved; what's
> relevant is whether or not you agreed to it.

Who forced anyone to use that Mock Court?  If you're not forced to use it,
by definition, you cannot be coerced by it.  (The difference between "Mock"
Court and "Real" Court is that Mock Courts have no power to coerce.)

Incidentally, to Libertarians "punishment" is not a relevant legal concept.
The goal of a legitimate legal system is restitution, not punishment;
ideally, to restore things to the way they would have been had the crime not
taken place.  

