Subject: Painless Privitisation

Things that you would have read if you'd read the Economist's November
28th profile on China:

"...in 1952, around 55% of Taiwan's value-added in manufacturing, and 90%
of industrial assets, were accounted for by state-owned firms.  Today
Taiwan's state-owned companies own 20% of industrial assets and account
for 10% of manufactured exports.  Taiwan accomplished this with virtually
no privitisation.  It simply grew the non-state businesses much faster."

If world economic activity experiences the explosive growth that we as
Extropians expect over the next two decades, the "coercive" sector can
be swamped simply by standing still.  Since the provision of goods and
services over the nets eliminates geographicand most physical restraints
on productivity, explosive growth can occur using the human resources
available anywhere in the world.

Recent growth rates in the financial services and telecommunications 
industries (already on the nets) provide evidence of the possibilities.

Next candidate for transfer to the nets: Entertainment

Then: sales and marketing (even of physical products)

After that: management and professional services

Once the coercive sector falls below some magic threshold as a percentage
of world economic activity, the world's governments become mere market 
participants increasingly unable to use the sword that sets them apart
from other market actors.

