Date: Wed, 30 Dec 1992 12:10:38 -0500
From: Elizabeth Schwartz <betsys@ra.cs.umb.edu>
Subject: The moral issues in creating intelligence: references?

It's not that Extropian, but for an interesting perspective, check out
Marge Piercy's book "He She and It" (Published overseas as "Body of
Glass." I'd describe this book as Jewish Feminist Cyberpunk. Set in a
future similar to William Gibson's in Neuromancer, a small "free town"
builds a cyborg to help defend themselves against data pirates and
corporate destroyers. The cyborg is built to be a weapon, but he also
has been given a safety check in the form of a conscience. The book
tells two stories in parallel: the story of the cyborg, and the story
of the Golem built in 16th century Prague to defend the Jews of that
town. 
  It's very interesting reading. Members of this list won't find new
technological ideas, but the moral issues raised are interesting. What
if the weapon doesn't WANT to kill? What if the AI sues for freedom?
(I think Asimov wrote a robot story where a robot petitioned for
freedom, but my memory is hazy...)
  Another cute SF story is "Valentina: Soul in Sapphire" by Joseph
Delany and Marc Steigler. An AI created as an Internet Worm wants to
live, but is being kicked out of computers everywhere. Among other
things, this AI ends up being incorporated in Texas and testifying in
court... 
  I have a small collection of science fiction about AI's and
programmers. I haven't read any "serious" work that extrapolates far
into the future of AI. I will be very interested in seeing your list!
thanks, Betsy


